// TEST: If Branch Types Must Agree (rejects)
// ERROR: BL3001
// ============================================================================
// The two branches of an `if` must already share a type. Blade has explicit
// numeric casts, and those are the licensed widening pathway (see 063); an
// implicit promotion at a site whose whole job is to CHOOSE between two
// values would convert one of them without the source ever saying so.
//
// A LITERAL DOES NOT ADAPT HERE. Both branches are inferred independently,
// so this `10` is an Int64 beside a Float64 and the pair is refused: write
// `10.0`. Adaptation belongs where a type is being checked against (an
// ascription, a declared parameter); an `if` has no expected type to push
// down, and taking one from whichever branch came first is exactly the
// order-dependence this check removes.
//
// The old arm discarded its branch unification and typed the node from the
// then-branch, so `if c then 2.5 else n` compiled to a C++ ternary that
// widened `n` silently, while the reverse order died inside g++ as a
// narrowing conversion with no Blade diagnostic at all.
let x = if false then 2.5 else 10
